lookup_method_return's 'External types: no const-qualifier metadata yet' is stale: 301 of 1002 Core method decls carry a trailing const in the decl string, unparsed


## Measured by lane-enum during B121, 2026-09-01

## The stale comment

`lookup_method_return` says *"External types: no const-qualifier metadata yet"*.
**That is no longer true, and it has been steering decisions.** It partly
underpinned B116's provenance argument for excluding the engine population.

## What the dumps actually carry

- **301 of 1002 Core class methods (30%) carry a trailing ` const` in their
  `decl` string** — e.g. `"string GetName() const"`. The constness is present,
  just unparsed.
- `returntypeflags` carries what `returntypedecl` loses: `decl "const T& opIndex(...)"`
  has `returntypedecl "T"` but `returntypeflags 7`. Histogram: `0` → 865 (value
  or handle), `3` → 123 (`T&`), `7` → 14 (`const T&`).

So by-value vs by-reference IS available. What is **absent at every level** is
the fresh-vs-aliasing distinction for a returned **handle**, which is why this
does not close B121.

## DO NOT "just parse the decl"

Parsing it would let B116's engine-receiver exclusion be revisited — that is the
prize — but it opens a **new false-positive surface**: 80 handle-returning
engine sites are reached across the fleet, and B121 measured that 3 of them
(`string::Split`, returning `string[]@` — a fresh array behind a handle) cannot
be separated from `Json::Value::Get`, which returns a genuine view. Both are
`returntypeflags 0` with a `@`.

**This needs its own variant sweep before any fix**, the same shape B116, B120
and B121 all used: keep the current answer, record every disagreement, read the
disagreements, then decide. Report EXPOSURE as well as emits — see the hazards
entry, because the live workspace surface has measured a structural zero three
times running and a fourth zero will mean nothing without reach.

## Related

`CoreMethod.is_const` is the dead field that would be the obvious place to put
this — see the sibling ticket. Fixing that and fixing this are the same work if
done in the right order, and the wrong order leaves a populated field nobody
reads.